A heavy flying animal needs a strong launch. Birds usually jump with their legs. But pterosaurs walked on all four limbs, with their wings folded up. In 2008, scientist Michael Habib suggested they used their powerful arms to vault into the air, like a pole vaulter, then flapped hard to climb.

Once airborne, Quetzalcoatlus probably soared on rising warm air, much as vultures and eagles do today, saving energy. Its wings were made of skin stiffened with fibres, stretched from one enormously long finger back to the legs. Muscles and blood vessels inside the wing let it change the wing's shape in flight. Some scientists think it could have covered huge distances in a single flight, though nobody can be sure.

{{story:how-birds-fly}}

An otter's body is shaped like a torpedo with legs. Its feet are webbed, and its thick tail works as a rudder. When it dives, it can close its ears and nostrils tight. Two layers of fur trap a blanket of air next to the skin, so the otter stays dry underneath even after an hour of swimming.

The best tool is on its face. Stiff whiskers pick up tiny ripples made by a moving fish, which helps in brown, muddy rivers where eyes are almost useless. Small-clawed otters hunt differently. Their claws are tiny and their fingers are nimble, so they feel under stones for crabs and snails, almost like you searching for a lost coin under a sofa.

{{story:sea-otters}}

The shell is usually tough plastic. On a bicycle helmet it is thin and light, while a motorcycle helmet has a thick shell to resist sharp objects and high-speed slides. The liner is the crushable foam. Comfort pads stop it rubbing your skin, but they do almost nothing in a crash.

The strap may be the most ignored part. An unfastened helmet can fly off before your head hits the ground. A good fit means two fingers between the strap and your chin, and the front edge sitting about two fingers above your eyebrows.

